WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.110
So now, let's talk about effort levels

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.110
of your agent. And those effort levels

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.710
you can actually find in your command

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.670
menu for Claude code. So if you open

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.790
that, then you can see here under model

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.390
that you can adjust the effort of your

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.510
model from low to medium to high to

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.990
extra high to max and ultra code. So

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.830
I'll dissect each one just so you

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.870
understand when you need to adjust these

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.750
and what is the difference between these

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.030
six. But first, let's quickly define

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.990
what effort is. Because in the agentic

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.510
world, effort in plain English is

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.150
essentially just how hard Claude thinks

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.710
before it acts. So if you select a low

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.630
effort, that's going to give you results

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.710
much faster with less token usage, but

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.430
it is also potentially less considered

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.790
and less in terms of quality. Higher

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.710
effort is slower, it does cost more, but

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.230
you will actually get a higher

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.150
probability that the quality of the

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.710
agent's output is much sharper. Now in

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.750
case you're interested about what really

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.750
happens under the hood for a harness

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.790
like Claude code, the effort level

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.829
simply adjusts what we are calling the

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:07.750
reasoning token budget for your specific

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.470
prompt. So if you select a higher

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.670
effort, Claude code under the hood will

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.510
just assign a higher number of tokens

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.790
for planning, for calling tools, for

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.550
doing stuff, basically. And if you

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.430
choose a lower budget, then it just

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.270
adjusts that budget to be the most

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.070
minimal that it can be. Now when

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.390
differentiating between these six

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.150
levels, the good news is between low,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.230
mid, high, extra high, and max, the

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.830
difference is very simple. If you are on

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.230
max effort, then you just have a higher

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.270
token budget for each of your prompts.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.110
If you are on the low effort, then you

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.430
have a much lower token budget. Now

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.390
Anthropic doesn't actually publish the

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.230
specific token budgets for each of these

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.590
effort levels, so it's more of a taste

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.950
component on which particular default

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.030
level you want to land depending on the

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.590
work that you do. For a lot of

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.510
developers, I do see them defaulting to

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.110
something like extra high or max simply

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.910
because of the deeper work that the

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.830
agent needs to do in order to complete

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.870
those tasks. But, if you're mainly doing

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.710
knowledge work, setting the default to

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.630
be mid or high is actually already good

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.630
enough for most. Now, to give you one

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.190
quick example, you can see here on the

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.830
left that I have effort set to low, and

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.510
here on the right I have effort set to

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.510
max. And I sent it the same prompt, and

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.990
right off the bat, you can see that for

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.430
one, this session is already done. While

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.870
this one at the right is still thinking

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.710
and is still creating that page. So,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.030
just from that, you can see that this

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.790
has more reasoning token budgets and is

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.949
actually a bit more deliberate around

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:39.070
creating this landing page for us. Apart

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.949
from that, if you actually look at the

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:43.350
thinking time of these two sessions, you

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.990
can see that this one that is set to low

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.550
effort is only thinking for 2 seconds at

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.990
a time or 1 second at a time, while this

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.430
one on the max effort is thinking at 5

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.630
seconds at a time. So, that is

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.990
essentially what effort is. It just

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.750
gives this session a bit more space to

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.390
think about its output before giving you

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.550
the final result. Now, a key point about

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.470
effort is that it doesn't actually

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.870
automatically give you better results.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.630
So, this really depends on the kind of

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.790
work that you do, but you can see here,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.710
this is the one created from low effort,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.229
and this is the one created on max

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:18.270
effort. And you can see on design, they

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.030
are quite similar. So, we will tackle

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.030
later on how we can improve these

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.350
designs so that they are unique and are

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.630
not looking like they are AI vibe coded.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.470
But, the point being that, especially on

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.229
design and creatives, and especially if

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:31.870
you're not dealing with code bases in

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.390
particular, it doesn't automatically

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.990
mean that low effort is less impressive

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.509
versus the max effort. And just to show

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:40.110
you the difference on the tokens

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.830
consumed by these two sessions, if I

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.270
actually type in context for each of

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.590
them, then you'll be able to inspect

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.270
exactly how many tokens across your 1

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.030
million token context window has already

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.590
been consumed. And you can see here that

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.150
under messages, which is the purple

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.630
area, which directly correlates with the

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.710
thinking time that this Claude agent has

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.510
done, the low effort consumed 20,000

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.110
tokens so far, while for the max effort

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.830
it consumed 27,000 tokens. So this

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.830
varies depending on your use case, but

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.630
the trend obviously is that if you are

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.510
set to max effort, then you'll consume

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.910
more tokens. This consumption is roughly

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.750
around 35 to 40% more than the low

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.110
effort result. Do we think that

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.710
consuming 40% more tokens is actually

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.750
worth it for the result that we got? I

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.310
think again, that depends on the work

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.110
that you do, but broadly at least from

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.190
my experience, setting it to mid or high

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.550
as default is actually more than good

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.190
enough for most knowledge work. Now

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.550
there is a sixth level, which was

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.510
recently introduced by Anthropic, and

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.630
this is the ultra code mode. And this is

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.390
a bit of an advanced topic actually, so

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.790
I advise you not to set it to ultra code

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.710
because it actually consumes the most

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.830
tokens amongst all of these effort

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.630
levels. But if you're curious on what

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.750
that does, ultra code basically spawns a

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.990
fleet of several agents so that they

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.230
work in parallel and cross checks

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.550
themselves before giving you the final

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.630
result. So if on the low to max effort

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.750
plans, you are actually just talking to

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.909
one agent, if you set it to ultra code,

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:14.950
then you're giving freedom to your agent

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.750
to spawn multiple agents in order to

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.870
tackle a broad task. So we'll talk more

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.310
about this in the coming courses, but

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.350
for now I think sticking to these five,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.270
using mid as a default and then just

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.190
adjusting up if in case you see that the

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.909
results you get are not the ones that

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:32.950
you would actually expect, would be my

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.880
advice to you.
